Evaluations of RAG

    PROs and CONs of RAG
        PROs:
        1.  Very quick to build, Rapid time to market
        2.  Quite scalable approach:
            > We can easily have 100 times as much data.
            > The ingestion might take sometime but that will be one time thing and we can get the vectorstore.
              Doing the query will be rally fast. We can tape into vast/huge expertise
        3.  Save on context length:
            > We don't need to fill the context with a lot of information. We just select the part that matters.
        4.  Avoid distractions with irrelevant context.

        CONs:
        1.  It's a hack
            > It's just a way to throw out the stuff PROBABLY, AND PROBABLY not important and focus on what is
              important.
        2.  "It's empirical" aka TRAIL and ERROR
            > You might think it works everytime but it doesn't work everytime. We have to do a lot of experiments to
              make it stable.
        3.  Unexpected I don't know
            > We might have the data related to the question similar like out previous day the question about IIOTY
              award. But we got I don't know. This can happen many time.
        4.  Great idea but we can have poor results.

    Case-in-point -> Chunking strategy can have a significant impaction performance.

    How do we convert this hack or art into Science.
    This is where the Evals come into play, i.e evaluations. Find a way to measure and quantitative the performance
    of our setup.

    |

    Evaluations:
        The simple technique to get performance from the our RAG pipeline
        Evaluations are everything.

---------------
Evaluations:

    Step 1: Curate a Test Set
    >   Is to build the golden set
    >   Curate a set of question and answers that are going to be our reference answers against which we are going to
        measure the performance
    >   One way of doing that is, We come up with set of questions and then for each one we can identity the few
        keywords that should be in the relevant contest that allows use to test the chunks we surface have these
        keyword in it.

    Step 2:
        There are 2 types of tests
        >   Measure Retrieval - We will measure how good is our retrieval
            Metrics like
                1. MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)
                2. nDCG (Normalized Discount Cumulative Gain)
                3. Recall@k
                4. Precision@k
        >   Measure Answers
            Use LLM-as-a-judge, to score provided answers against criteria like accuracy, completeness and relevance

    1. MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)
        > Average inverse rank of first hit;
        score:
        1       if the first chunk always has relevant context
        1/2     if the relevant context is in 2nd chunk
        1/3     if tha relevant context is in 3rd chunk
        1/4     if the relevant context is in 4th chunk
        .
        .
        .
        .
        so on...

        And we take average of if and that's called MRR

    2. nDCG (Normalized Discount Cumulative Gain)
        > It's not just looking at the first hit, it's looking at all the hits and asks the question how well
          distributed are these hits in these chunks.
        > Does it tend to be that the chunks that are relevant are the first few chunks? or does they come at lower
          down? So it's more about the distribution of chunks in our results
        > If all of the chunks that had relevant context are all top few once then we will score a perfect 1 with nDCG

    3. Recall@k - k is a number like Recall@3 or Recall@4
        > What % of out tests if we takes top k chunks was it a hit? There was relevant context in those top k chunks?
        > If the score is 50% for Recall@k -> That means, In half of the cases, the top k chunks had relevant context
          somewhere in them.
        > Or if you have multiple keywords to look for keyword coverage is similar to recall metric.

    4. Precision@k
        > What proportion of the chinks that are surfaced had relevant context in them?
        > If we are always surfacing k chunks, then on avg how many chunks of those k chunks have relevant context.
        > If the score is 50% for Precision@k -> That means, In about half the chunks that are surfaced in each of our
          RAG, we are surfacing relevant context.
          Even if it's true of all the test cases, but our precision if only 50%. That means, in each case it's only
          half of top k that are getting a hit in the chunks.

